### Also seen in Bash: `case` (not in the course notebooks)

You may meet `case` elsewhere. It compares one word against several glob patterns:

```bash
case $answer in
    y|yes) echo "OK" ;;
    n|no)  echo "cancelled" ;;
    *)     echo "please answer yes or no" ;;
esac
```

The course notebooks never use it, so you do not need it for the midterm; an `if`/`elif` chain with `[[ $answer == ... ]]` does the same job.
